+# Build check() and consensus() — Step C of "agent work on every page"
+
+Task dir (read this whole conversation, not a summary): `public/framework/ai/2026-09-28/check-consensus/`
+Design doc (④ and ⑤): `public/framework/ai/2026/09/28/agent-work-on-every-page-sanity-checks-c/design.md`
+Owner's words (verbatim, the actual ask): `public/framework/ai/2026/09/28/agent-work-on-every-page-sanity-checks-c/owner-words.md`
+Reuse pattern (read it first, in full): `Server/review.mjs` — the same three-call spawn (`spawn_agent` → `wait_for_agent` → `stop_agent` over the loopback MCP at `http://127.0.0.1:8090/mcp`) that "fresh eyes" review already uses. Do not fork review.mjs; write new files that reuse its pattern.
+
+You are a minion. Load the `minion` skill first, then `code` before writing any JS.
+
+## What to build
+
+**1. `Server/check.mjs`** — a node module exporting `async function check(claim, context)`.
+- `claim`: the one sentence being checked (a decision, an answer, a landing outcome).
+- `context`: `{ ownerWords: [file paths], files: [file paths the claim is about], cardDir: path to the card's directory (so it can append the result there) }`.
+- Spawns 2 fresh Servex agents from different model families — Haiku (`claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`) and Sonnet (`claude-sonnet-5`) today, so a note in the code says "OpenRouter adds more families once harness step 2 lands." Each checker gets ONLY the claim, the owner's words file(s), and the files context names — never the caller's reasoning or conversation. Ask it: could this be wrong, what are the alternatives, agree or doubt — answer in 5 lines at most, first line exactly `verdict: agree` or `verdict: doubt`.
+- Parse each checker's reply into `{model, verdict: "agree"|"doubt", why}`.
+- Cost: sum both checkers' `wait_for_agent` cost (same field `review.mjs` reads: `waited.cost`).
+- Append one line to `<cardDir>/task.jsonl` (create the file if it doesn't exist, same append style as `Server/review.mjs`'s `appendJSON`): `{"check":{"at":ISO, "claim":claim, "verdicts":[...], "cost":totalCost}}`.
+- Export a `chip(verdicts)` helper that returns `"✓✓"` when both agree, `"✓?"` when they split or either doubts — this is what a card shows; keep it simple, a two-line function.
+- CLI usage for testing: `node Server/check.mjs <cardDir> <claim> [--owner-words <file>] [--files <file,file>]`. Print the result as JSON and the chip.
+- Every child process spawn sets `windowsHide: true` (there are none directly here — the spawning is over MCP `fetch`, same as review.mjs's `mcp()` helper; reuse that exact function, copied in, not imported, since review.mjs isn't a library entry point).
+- Never throws: a Servex problem becomes a `{"verdict":"doubt","why":"checker failed to run: <msg>"}` for that checker, not a crash.
+
+**2. `Server/consensus.mjs`** — a node module exporting `async function consensus(question, options)`.
+- `question`: the open question. `options`: the list of choices (strings).
+- 5 models vote, at 3 price levels. Use the Claude tiers today (OpenRouter is NOT live — harness step 2 hasn't landed): e.g. 2x Haiku-tier, 2x Sonnet-tier, 1x Opus-tier (`claude-opus-5-5`) — comment in the code that this becomes OpenRouter models, chosen from one config table with prices, once step 2 lands. Put the 5-model roster in one small exported config array (`VOTERS`) so swapping to OpenRouter later is a one-array edit, not a rewrite.
+- Each voter gets the question and options, answers with exactly one option (first line `pick: <option>`) plus one line: the one improvement it would add to the winner (`caveat: ...`).
+- Vote shape is `{member, pick, caveat}` — NOT `{model, vote, why}` — per the design doc's "Added" section (Collab system, Vote row: "a favourite plus the one improvement it would add; the winner absorbs the caveats"), so `consensus`'s output is directly reusable by task-mastermind-collab-rounds. `member` is the voter's model/id.
+- Tally the votes (by `pick`), append `{"consensus":{"at":ISO, "question":question, "options":options, "votes":[{member,pick,caveat}], "tally":{option:count}, "cost":totalCost}}` to `<cardDir>/task.jsonl` the same way.
+- CLI usage: `node Server/consensus.mjs <cardDir> "<question>" "<option1>" "<option2>" ...`. Print result JSON and the tally.
+- Same never-throws rule as check.mjs.
+
+**3. Servex tool `check`** — new file `Servex/agents/check-tools.js`, same shape as `Servex/agents/ops.js` (`const tool = (name, description, properties, required, handler) => ({...})`, export a `check_tools(agents)` function — `agents` unused today but keep the parameter for the same reason ops.js does, consistency). Two tools: `check` (args: `cardDir`, `claim`, `ownerWords?`, `files?`) and `consensus` (args: `cardDir`, `question`, `options`) — both just call the modules above and return their JSON result. Wire it into `Servex/agents/tools.js`: import `check_tools`, add `...check_tools(agents)` to the array `tools()` returns (see the existing `...ops_tools(agents)` line — same pattern).
+
+**4. One line in the sub-mastermind skill** — `.claude/skills/sub-mastermind/SKILL.md`. Find a natural spot (near where it talks about decisions/landing, or add a short new paragraph) and add ONE line saying: `check()` runs by itself after a manager or task mastermind writes a `decision` line, and on a landing outcome — never on the fast assistant's quick replies. Keep it to one line, matching the terse style already in that file. Do not restructure the file.
+
+**5. Docs** — `Server/doc/check.md`, one screen (the `documentation` skill's standard: what it is, how it's used, a link to the design). Load the `documentation` skill before writing it.
+
+## Fence
+
+You may create/edit only: `Server/check.mjs`, `Server/consensus.mjs`, `Servex/agents/check-tools.js`, `Servex/agents/tools.js` (one import + one array-spread line only), `.claude/skills/sub-mastermind/SKILL.md` (one line only), `Server/doc/check.md`. Nothing else. If you think another file needs a change, stop and say so instead of editing it.
+
+## Cost target
+
+Measured, not guessed: a `check()` call should cost under $0.01 (2 short Haiku/Sonnet turns), a `consensus()` call under $0.05 (5 short turns, one Opus). If your test run comes in over, say so plainly in your landing note rather than hiding it — the task mastermind will decide whether that's acceptable.
+
+## When you're done
+
+Run both CLIs once for real, against a real claim (see the task dir's requirements.md — "5 real decisions from today's task logs" is the proof the task mastermind still owes; you don't have to find those 5, just prove your own code runs once). Report back with: what you built, the fence you touched, your one real test run's output (paste the chip/tally and the cost), and anything you couldn't finish.
